# ember

A read-only alert reporter for a single server. Ember polls host and Docker
metrics, evaluates threshold alerts, has an LLM agent triage what fired (using
sandboxed shell and read-only Docker tools), and sends a structured report to
your sinks. Nothing listens for replies — every sink is one-way.

Everything is configured from a small web UI: alert rules, the agent, sinks,
and the agent's prompts. Recent reports show up there too.

## Run

    docker compose up -d --build

then open http://localhost:7340. On a remote server, use an SSH tunnel
(`ssh -L 7340:localhost:7340 yourserver`) or put tailscale/caddy/etc. in front.
If you reach it through another hostname, add it to `EMBER_ALLOWED_HOSTS` in
`docker-compose.yml`; requests with any other `Host` header are rejected.

Ember has no login of its own. The port is published on `127.0.0.1` only —
keep it that way unless something with auth sits in front, since
docker-published ports bypass ufw.

Or locally (UI on http://localhost:8080, data in `./data`):

    cd frontend && bun install && bun run build && cd ..
    uv run python main.py

## Sinks

- **discord** — webhook. Create one in your notification channel
  (Settings → Integrations → Webhooks). Reports arrive as embeds,
  color-coded by severity.
- **email** — plain SMTP, STARTTLS by default.

With no sink enabled, reports still land in the UI's history.

## Data

Config, prompt overrides and the last 50 reports live in one sqlite file in
the `ember-data` volume. It survives rebuilds and image deletion; only
`docker compose down -v` removes it. Settings has an export/import button for
backups — exports never contain secrets (API key, webhook URL, SMTP password).

## Prompts

The agent's instructions are the `*.md` files in `prompts/`, concatenated in
filename order. Each can be overridden from the UI and reset back to the
default.

## Security

The container runs with a read-only root filesystem, a bounded `noexec` `/tmp`,
dropped capabilities (keeping only `SETUID`, `SETGID`, and `KILL`),
`no-new-privileges`, pid/memory limits, and `/` mounted read-only at `/host`.
The Docker socket is mounted inside a root-only directory (`/run/ember`, mode
`0700`) so the daemon and read-only `docker_*` tools can use it, while shell
commands run as UID `10001` with a clean environment.

The web UI refuses connections from inside the container, so shell commands
can't use it. A path back in through the host (the port published on
`0.0.0.0`, or a reverse proxy without auth) would look like a normal client;
the default `127.0.0.1` publish doesn't have one.

Shell commands can still read anything world-readable on the host through
`/host`, see host process command lines, and make network connections. Don't
keep secrets world-readable or in command-line arguments, and keep the host
Docker socket at its default permissions (`0660 root:docker`): if it is
world-writable, shell commands can reach it at `/host/var/run/docker.sock`.

## Tests

    uv run pytest

Tests mock external calls; no API keys, Docker daemon, or notification services required.
